            <ArticleTitle>The effect of pentoxifylline on existing hypersensitivity in a rat model of neuropathy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>650-3, table of contents</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1213/ane.0b013e31815efaba</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Using a rat L5 spinal nerve transection model we previously showed that pentoxifylline prevents hyperalgesia through antiinflammation in the prefrontal brain. In this study, we examined efficacy when applied after injury.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We examined the effect of pentoxifylline on existing mechanical allodynia, observing glial activation and proinflammatory cytokine expression in the lumbar spinal cord, when given 7 days after L5 spinal nerve transection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no effect from pentoxifylline on existing hypersensitivity, glial activation, and cytokine expression when applied after L5 spinal nerve transection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Pentoxifylline administered intraperitoneally on day 7 postsurgery failed to alleviate existing hypersensitivity, or reduce glial activation and cytokine expression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
